<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hospices de Beaune</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1443</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hospices de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaune">Beaune</a> is a former charitable hospital in Beaune, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, founded in 1443 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-rolin">Nicolas Rolin</a>, chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, and his wife Guigone de Salins. Its Hôtel-Dieu, with a roof of glazed coloured tiles, is a masterpiece of late Gothic architecture, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogier-van-der-weyden">Rogier van der Weyden</a>'s Last Judgement altarpiece was painted for it. The Hospices still own vineyards whose wine is sold at a famous annual charity auction.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hospices-de-beaune.jpg" alt="Hospices de Beaune" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beaune_-_H%C3%B4tel-Dieu_-_Cour_-_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
